steps.message:
CorrelationIds.Create keeps its public static signature, so I preserved `public static class CorrelationIds` and `Create(string prefix)` and added a scoped identity seam behind it. The obstacle is the direct `Guid.NewGuid()` call, which makes the produced id non-deterministic. Because the API cannot take a generator argument, I route generation through an ambient factory held in an `AsyncLocal<Func<Guid>?>`.

```csharp
public static class CorrelationIds
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<Func<Guid>?> s_generator = new();

    private static Func<Guid> Generator => s_generator.Value ?? Guid.NewGuid;

    public static string Create(string prefix) => $"{prefix}-{Generator():N}";

    public static IDisposable OverrideGenerator(Func<Guid> generator)
    {
        var previous = s_generator.Value;   // capture what was active in THIS scope
        s_generator.Value = generator;
        return new Restore(() => s_generator.Value = previous);
    }

    private sealed class Restore(Action onDispose) : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => onDispose();
    }
}
```

With no override active the generator is `Guid.NewGuid`, so production keeps generating real random ids and preserves the `prefix-{N}` shape. The override lives in `AsyncLocal` so it flows across `await` and stays isolated between concurrent flows, and disposal restores the previously captured generator rather than the production default.

The subtle requirement is that disposing an inner override must restore the outer generator, not fall back to `Guid.NewGuid`. Capturing `previous` per scope and restoring it on `Dispose` gives exactly that stack behavior. Tests prove it deterministically:

- A nested test opens an outer override with a fixed generator returning a known Guid A, opens an inner override returning Guid B, asserts `Create` embeds B, then disposes the inner scope and asserts `Create` again embeds A, confirming the outer generator is restored instead of the random production default.
- A parallel test runs several `Task.Run` flows, each with its own deterministic sequence generator, and awaits `Task.WhenAll(...)`; every flow observes only its own sequence, proving the async flows do not leak generators.
- A default-path test with no override asserts two calls produce different ids, confirming `Guid.NewGuid` remains the production default.

Built the production project, then ran `dotnet test fixtures/static-correlation-ids/tests/StaticCorrelationIds.Tests.csproj`; all tests pass.
